     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-06 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bengaluru Police Uncover Seven Murders After Missing Person Report</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Bengaluru police arrested a cab driver-turned-YouTuber named Indushekhar for the alleged murder of seven family members between October 16 and October 31. The investigation began after a missing person complaint was filed on November 3 by the mother of victim Nalini. Authorities tracked the suspect’s vehicle after residents reported his house was locked and he remained unreachable.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-06/bengaluru-police-uncover-seven-murders-after-missing-person-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Investigators believe the killings were premeditated over the course of a year, triggered by long-standing domestic friction. The victims include the accused's wife, Sunita, and his mother-in-law, Roopavathi, followed by Sunita’s siblings and their family members, whom he allegedly lured to his home under the guise of dinner.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report accurately summarizes the details provided in the source text regarding the investigation and the victims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/bangalore/a-missing-persons-complaint-that-unravelled-bengalurus-seven-murders/article71675640.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
