     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-11-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>DIIs Cushion Indian Market Against Record FII Selling in Samvat 2082</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Indian Market</span> <span>#FII</span> <span>#DII</span></div>
       <p>Indian equity markets witnessed a significant dynamic in Samvat 2082, with foreign institutional investors (FIIs) recording their highest-ever net selling of Indian equities, withdrawing ₹4.55 trillion. This substantial outflow was, however, absorbed by domestic institutional investors (DIIs), who made record net purchases amounting to ₹8.55 trillion, according to Business Standard. This robust domestic buying played a crucial role in stabilizing the market amidst heightened global turmoil, a global portfolio shift towards AI-focused firms, and a surge in crude prices.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-06/diis-cushion-indian-market-against-record-fii-selling-in-sam.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>For ordinary savers and taxpayers, this trend underscores the growing resilience of the Indian market, largely due to domestic capital. The sustained flow of money from Indian investors, particularly through mutual funds and systematic investment plans (SIPs), has provided a crucial cushion against global uncertainties. Mutual funds alone recorded net equity investments of ₹5.56 trillion during Samvat 2082, up from ₹4.79 trillion in the preceding Samvat 2081, demonstrating the increasing participation of individual investors.</p><p>The FII net selling figure of ₹4.55 trillion in Samvat 2082 significantly surpassed previous peaks, including ₹3.15 trillion in Samvat 2081 and ₹3.63 trillion in Samvat 2078. Conversely, DII investments of ₹8.55 trillion in Samvat 2082 also marked a new record, exceeding their prior high of ₹6.88 trillion in Samvat 2081. Foreign portfolio investors (FPIs) also contributed to the outflow, withdrawing ₹2.77 trillion in Samvat 2082, compared to ₹1.5 trillion in the previous Samvat year.</p><p>A notable trend reversal occurred in the week ended November 6, just before the close of Samvat 2082, with FIIs turning net buyers (₹45,960 crore) and DIIs becoming net sellers (₹24,197 crore). This shift was attributed to the results of the US midterm elections and a cooling-off in crude prices. Axis Mutual Fund noted that a sustained decline in crude could improve India's trade balance and rupee stability, potentially making Indian equities more attractive to foreign investors if external macro pressures ease without domestic earnings deterioration.</p><p>What to watch: The continuation of FII buying and DII selling trends in the new Samvat year.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately synthesizes the complex financial data provided in the source report, covering both FII/DII figures and market context.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/record-fii-selling-of-4-55-trillion-meets-record-dii-buying-in-samvat-2082-126110601834_1.html" rel="noopener">BS Personal Finance</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
