     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-11-06 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>EU Accuses TikTok's 'Rabbit Hole' Algorithm of Breaching Content Rules</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#EU</span> <span>#TikTok</span> <span>#Digital Services Act</span> <span>#Algorithm</span></div>
       <p>The European Commission has accused TikTok of breaching the Digital Services Act (DSA) over the potentially harmful effects of its recommendation system on the 'For You' page. The European Union's regulatory arm stated that the personalized nature of TikTok's algorithm means users are repeatedly exposed to similar content based on their interactions, creating what it terms &quot;rabbit holes.&quot;</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-06/eu-accuses-tiktoks-rabbit-hole-algorithm-of-breaching-conten.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>In its preliminary findings, the Commission highlighted concerns that &quot;This design can be harmful when such endless and repeated exposure involves, for example, content about sadness, isolation or body image.&quot; The regulator warned that these &quot;rabbit holes&quot; could detrimentally impact users' mental health, particularly vulnerable adults and minors. The Commission found TikTok had not adequately assessed how its algorithm could draw users into these potentially risky content loops, nor did it implement sufficient measures to mitigate such risks, noting that a user tapping &quot;not interested&quot; on a video &quot;may not be enough to change the content feed.&quot;</p><p>The EU has mandated that TikTok redesign its 'For You' page to rely more on users' explicit preferences, disable viewing time as a content recommendation indicator, enhance the accessibility and usability of user controls, and regularly test its algorithm. Should TikTok fail to resolve these issues to the Commission's satisfaction and the findings are confirmed, its parent company, ByteDance, could face a fine of up to six percent of its annual global revenue.</p><p>TikTok has refuted the allegations, stating to Reuters, &quot;The Commission has made a series of statements that are fundamentally inaccurate and distort our platform's architecture and our commitment to user safety.&quot; The company maintained it has &quot;a strong record of protecting our community while giving users direct and intuitive control over their content experience.&quot; These preliminary findings are part of a broader EU investigation into TikTok, which commenced in early 2024. The bloc previously cited TikTok for breaching the DSA in February over its &quot;addictive design&quot; and in July for failing to protect the privacy of minors' accounts. Regulators globally are intensifying scrutiny on social media companies regarding their platforms' impact on children, with Google, Meta, and TikTok recently suing California over a law banning &quot;addictive features&quot; for users under 16.</p><p>What to watch: TikTok's response to the EU's demands and potential fines.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft provides a comprehensive summary of the EU's investigation into TikTok, correctly identifying the core allegations and potential consequences.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2309300/the-eu-accuses-tiktoks-rabbit-hole-algorithm-of-breaching-its-content-rules/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
