     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Hyderabad Startup Unveils India's First Private Space Debris Recovery Facility</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Space Tech</span> <span>#Debris Recovery</span> <span>#Satellite Servicing</span> <span>#Hyderabad</span></div>
       <p>Hyderabad-based startup Cosmoserve Space on Thursday unveiled 'Base 1', touted as India’s first private facility for satellite servicing and space debris recovery. The facility, also only the sixth in the world with space-vision systems and microgravity simulation capabilities, was inaugurated by Union Minister of State for Science &amp; Technology and Space Dr. Jitendra Singh and Telangana IT and Industries Minister D. Sridhar Babu.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-06/hyderabad-startup-unveils-indias-first-private-space-debris-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Spread across multiple specialised laboratories, Base 1 combines satellite integration, space robotics, mission simulation, and orbital capture technologies. This integration enables Cosmoserve Space to design, build, test, and operate satellite-servicing missions. The facility includes an Orbital Rendezvous and Capture Lab, a Space Robotics Innovation Lab, an ISO Class 8 clean room for satellite integration, and a Mission Simulation and Control Centre. Beyond space debris recovery, the centre will focus on in-orbit satellite repairs, refuelling, and maintenance services.</p><p>Dr. Chiranjeevi Phanindra, Cosmoserve founder and CEO, highlighted the global space industry's challenge of orbital debris. Of the nearly 28,000 satellites currently in orbit, approximately 10,000 are non-operational, with millions of debris fragments posing risks to active satellites and space missions. Dr. Jitendra Singh congratulated the company, noting the facility reflects the growing technological capabilities of India's private space startups, while Mr. Sridhar Babu remarked on Hyderabad's emergence as a major spacetech hub with over 30 companies in related fields.</p><p>Cosmoserve has developed a proprietary Dual Spacecraft Architecture, comprising a larger Orbital Service Station and a smaller Autonomous Service Vehicle, designed to service multiple satellites and remove debris at significantly lower costs. The company estimates this technology could reduce satellite-servicing costs from around $10 million to less than $1 million per satellite. Following a recent $6-million seed funding round, bringing its total funding to over $9 million, Cosmoserve plans to test its robotic capture systems in microgravity flights, re-fly its Mission Embrace payload, and conduct a full-scale space-debris capture demonstration by 2027.</p><p>What to watch: Future demonstrations of robotic capture systems and debris removal by 2027.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively integrates all key facts from the source, including the facility's name, purpose, capabilities, and funding.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/telangana/hyderabad-startup-opens-indias-first-private-space-debris-recovery-facility/article71670668.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       <div class="cityguide"><b>City guide</b><p>More on <a href="https://bharatdn.com/city/hyderabad/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyderabad</a> - census 2011 population, live weather and air quality, local food and getaways.</p></div>
