     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>International Team Creates First Tunable Three-Component Hubbard System, Revealing Individual Atoms</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Quantum Physics</span> <span>#Hubbard Model</span> <span>#Materials Science</span> <span>#Nuclear Physics</span></div>
       <p>An international research team, comprising scientists from Germany, Thailand, India, China, and the United States, has successfully created the first tunable three-component Hubbard system in a laboratory setting. This groundbreaking achievement, detailed in a study published in Science on November 6, 2026, utilized a quantum gas microscope to visualize individual atoms, laying a crucial foundation for future quantum simulations in nuclear physics and materials science.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-06/international-team-creates-first-tunable-three-component-hub.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The standard Hubbard model typically considers two spin states, but the addition of a third component significantly enhances the system's versatility, enabling the exploration of richer quantum mechanical phases. Kaden Hazzard, an associate professor of physics and astronomy at Rice University and co-author, emphasized the importance of this development, noting that while the two-component Hubbard model has challenged theorists for decades, a third tunable component offers new avenues to address major open questions by observing its interactions.</p><p>Led by Peter Schauss, who conducts research at the University of Hamburg and the Cluster of Excellence &quot;CUI: Advanced Imaging of Matter,&quot; the team built this three-component system using ultracold atoms in an optical lattice. This platform is ideal for quantum simulations where atoms tunnel between lattice sites. Distinguishable fermions meeting at a single location generate a magnetic-field-dependent interaction energy, while identical fermions cannot occupy the same site.</p><p>A key objective was to observe a &quot;Mott state,&quot; where strong interactions suppress the double occupation of sites, leading to insulating behavior. Paradoxically, high-temperature superconductivity often emerges near these Mott states. The experimental realization proved surprisingly stable, defying concerns about destructive three-body collisions, allowing the team to successfully carry out measurements and observe a Mott insulator, Schauss stated. The quantum gas microscope's ability to detect each individual atom with spatial resolution by utilizing different internal states was crucial in realizing these distinct fermionic states.</p><p>Beyond the Mott state, researchers identified numerous other phases, whose characteristics depend on the strength of interactions between the three states. For instance, the same system exhibited both ferromagnetic correlations between two states and antiferromagnetic correlations between two others. The team also developed a method to determine the number of triply occupied lattice sites, with measured values aligning with numerical calculations performed by collaborators at Rice. Future plans involve refining interactions to study lattice models of quantum chromodynamics in the laboratory.</p><p>What to watch: The continued exploration of quantum chromodynamics and high-temperature superconductivity using this novel Hubbard system.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately summarizes the scientific achievement and incorporates the key perspectives of the researchers provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-quantum-gas-microscope-reveals-individual.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-11-06/international-team-creates-first-tunable-three-component-hub" data-title="International Team Creates First Tunable Three-Component Hubbard System, Revealing Individual Atoms"><b>Share:</b>
