     <article class="story single" data-beat="health">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">HEALTH</span> · BBC Health · 2026-11-06 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Wales Advances Plan to Ban Energy Drink Sales to Under-16s Amid Health Concerns</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Health</span> <span>#Wales</span> <span>#Energy Drinks</span> <span>#Youth Policy</span></div>
       <p>The Welsh government is moving forward with proposals to prohibit the sale of high-caffeine energy drinks to children under 16, citing a growing body of evidence linking their consumption to adverse health outcomes. This follows broad support from both young people and health experts, with Public and Preventative Health Minister Nerys Evans stating that previous consultations showed &quot;broad support for action.&quot; England has already established a similar ban, set to take effect from April, making it illegal to sell drinks like Red Bull, Monster, Relentless, and Prime to under-16s if they contain more than 150mg of caffeine per litre. Lower-caffeine soft drinks, tea, and coffee are not affected by these proposed restrictions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-06/wales-advances-plan-to-ban-energy-drink-sales-to-under-16s-a.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Minister Evans highlighted that the latest evidence indicates a link between high-caffeine energy drink intake and negative health consequences in children, including increased frequency of headaches, irritation, tiredness, and stomach aches. It also points to reduced sleep duration and quality, alongside an increased risk of stress, anxiety, and depression. Experts emphasize that young people can be more sensitive to caffeine due to their smaller bodies and developing brains, with some popular brands containing caffeine levels equivalent to more than two cups of coffee or four cans of cola. The Association of UK Dieticians also links sugary versions of these drinks to obesity and damaged teeth, while rare cases of rapid heart rate, abnormal rhythms, and seizures have been connected to excessive caffeine.</p><p>Young people in Cardiff, interviewed by BBC Wales, expressed their backing for the proposed ban. Vee, 17, shared that it was &quot;really easy to start relying on them,&quot; while Sunny, 16, advised opting for &quot;just have a coffee&quot; instead. However, the British Soft Drinks Association (BSDA) has voiced opposition, calling the proposed ban &quot;unnecessary and not supported by robust evidence of overconsumption or harm.&quot; The BSDA also noted that its members have voluntarily not marketed or promoted energy drinks to under-16s since 2010, and all high-caffeine beverages already carry a label warning they are &quot;not recommended for children.&quot; They contend that the &quot;vast majority of caffeine consumed by children and adolescents comes from sources other than energy drinks.&quot;</p><p>Aisling Pigott, a nutritionist and registered dietician in Cardiff, welcomed the proposed ban, though she considered it &quot;10 years too late,&quot; given Wales' &quot;disproportionate concerns around diet and lifestyle.&quot; Pigott likened caffeine's effect to &quot;going into your overdraft,&quot; providing immediate energy followed by a &quot;crash.&quot; She also raised concerns about children consuming these drinks instead of nutritious food, using the sugar and caffeine as an &quot;easier access to give energy.&quot; The current Welsh government intends to proceed with the prohibition, subject to the outcome of an ongoing consultation, which seeks views on the proposed &quot;enforcement framework,&quot; including online age-verification, compliance, sanctions, implementation timescales, and monitoring arrangements.</p><p>What to watch: The final outcome of the Welsh government's consultation on the prohibition and its enforcement.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive coverage of the proposed ban, including expert opinions, government rationale, and industry opposition.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cx4g114r0d2do?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC Health</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
